1
00:00:02,559 --> 00:00:05,919
Chapter 20.

2
00:00:05,919 --> 00:00:10,840
The journey from our town to the metropolis was a journey of about five hours. It was a

3
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:15,319
little past midday when the four-horse stage-coach by which I was a passenger,

4
00:00:15,319 --> 00:00:21,879
got into the ravel of traffic frayed out about the Cross Keys, Wood Street, Cheapside, London.

5
00:00:21,879 --> 00:00:26,559
We Britons had at that time particularly settled that it was treasonable to doubt our having and

6
00:00:26,559 --> 00:00:33,680
our being the best of everything: otherwise, while I was scared by the immensity of London, I think

7
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:41,039
I might have had some faint doubts whether it was not rather ugly, crooked, narrow, and dirty.

8
00:00:41,039 --> 00:00:47,199
Mr. Jaggers had duly sent me his address; it was, Little Britain, and he had written after it on his

9
00:00:47,199 --> 00:00:54,199
card, “just out of Smithfield, and close by the coach-office.” Nevertheless, a hackney-coachman,

10
00:00:54,199 --> 00:00:59,039
who seemed to have as many capes to his greasy great-coat as he was years old,

11
00:00:59,039 --> 00:01:03,919
packed me up in his coach and hemmed me in with a folding and jingling barrier of steps,

12
00:01:03,919 --> 00:01:09,000
as if he were going to take me fifty miles. His getting on his box, which I remember to have

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,478
been decorated with an old weather-stained pea-green hammercloth moth-eaten into rags,

14
00:01:14,478 --> 00:01:21,278
was quite a work of time. It was a wonderful equipage, with six great coronets outside,

15
00:01:21,278 --> 00:01:25,598
and ragged things behind for I don’t know how many footmen to hold on by,

16
00:01:25,599 --> 00:01:31,638
and a harrow below them, to prevent amateur footmen from yielding to the temptation.

17
00:01:31,638 --> 00:01:37,080
I had scarcely had time to enjoy the coach and to think how like a straw-yard it was, and yet how

18
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:43,000
like a rag-shop, and to wonder why the horses’ nose-bags were kept inside, when I observed the

19
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,240
coachman beginning to get down, as if we were going to stop presently. And stop we presently

20
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:57,919
did, in a gloomy street, at certain offices with an open door, whereon was painted MR. JAGGERS.

21
00:01:57,919 --> 00:02:00,559
“How much?” I asked the coachman.

22
00:02:00,559 --> 00:02:05,759
The coachman answered, “A shilling—unless you wish to make it more.”

23
00:02:05,759 --> 00:02:09,080
I naturally said I had no wish to make it more.

24
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:13,438
“Then it must be a shilling,” observed the coachman. “I don’t want to get into trouble.

25
00:02:13,438 --> 00:02:21,558
I know him!” He darkly closed an eye at Mr. Jaggers’s name, and shook his head.

26
00:02:21,559 --> 00:02:26,159
When he had got his shilling, and had in course of time completed the ascent to his box,

27
00:02:26,159 --> 00:02:30,118
and had got away (which appeared to relieve his mind), I went into the

28
00:02:30,118 --> 00:02:35,319
front office with my little portmanteau in my hand and asked, Was Mr. Jaggers at home?

29
00:02:35,319 --> 00:02:40,758
“He is not,” returned the clerk. “He is in Court at present. Am I addressing Mr. Pip?”

30
00:02:40,759 --> 00:02:43,680
I signified that he was addressing Mr. Pip.

31
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:48,759
“Mr. Jaggers left word, would you wait in his room. He couldn’t say how long he might be,

32
00:02:48,759 --> 00:02:51,798
having a case on. But it stands to reason,

33
00:02:51,798 --> 00:02:55,798
his time being valuable, that he won’t be longer than he can help.”

34
00:02:55,798 --> 00:02:59,919
With those words, the clerk opened a door, and ushered me into an inner chamber at the

35
00:02:59,919 --> 00:03:05,638
back. Here, we found a gentleman with one eye, in a velveteen suit and knee-breeches,

36
00:03:05,638 --> 00:03:11,159
who wiped his nose with his sleeve on being interrupted in the perusal of the newspaper.

37
00:03:11,159 --> 00:03:14,719
“Go and wait outside, Mike,” said the clerk.

38
00:03:14,719 --> 00:03:17,478
I began to say that I hoped I was not interrupting,

39
00:03:17,478 --> 00:03:23,478
when the clerk shoved this gentleman out with as little ceremony as I ever saw used,

40
00:03:23,478 --> 00:03:28,599
and tossing his fur cap out after him, left me alone.

41
00:03:28,599 --> 00:03:35,438
Mr. Jaggers’s room was lighted by a skylight only, and was a most dismal place; the skylight,

42
00:03:35,438 --> 00:03:40,878
eccentrically pitched like a broken head, and the distorted adjoining houses looking as if

43
00:03:40,879 --> 00:03:46,520
they had twisted themselves to peep down at me through it. There were not so many papers about,

44
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:51,359
as I should have expected to see; and there were some odd objects about, that I should not have

45
00:03:51,359 --> 00:03:58,598
expected to see,—such as an old rusty pistol, a sword in a scabbard, several strange-looking

46
00:03:58,599 --> 00:04:05,199
boxes and packages, and two dreadful casts on a shelf, of faces peculiarly swollen,

47
00:04:05,199 --> 00:04:12,637
and twitchy about the nose. Mr. Jaggers’s own high-backed chair was of deadly black horsehair,

48
00:04:12,638 --> 00:04:18,559
with rows of brass nails round it, like a coffin; and I fancied I could see how he leaned back in

49
00:04:18,559 --> 00:04:24,240
it, and bit his forefinger at the clients. The room was but small, and the clients seemed to have

50
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:30,240
had a habit of backing up against the wall; the wall, especially opposite to Mr. Jaggers’s chair,

51
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:35,278
being greasy with shoulders. I recalled, too, that the one-eyed gentleman had

52
00:04:35,278 --> 00:04:40,958
shuffled forth against the wall when I was the innocent cause of his being turned out.

53
00:04:40,958 --> 00:04:46,719
I sat down in the cliental chair placed over against Mr. Jaggers’s chair, and became fascinated

54
00:04:46,719 --> 00:04:52,599
by the dismal atmosphere of the place. I called to mind that the clerk had the same air of knowing

55
00:04:52,599 --> 00:04:59,039
something to everybody else’s disadvantage, as his master had. I wondered how many other

56
00:04:59,039 --> 00:05:04,477
clerks there were upstairs, and whether they all claimed to have the same detrimental mastery of

57
00:05:04,478 --> 00:05:10,359
their fellow-creatures. I wondered what was the history of all the odd litter about the room,

58
00:05:10,359 --> 00:05:16,838
and how it came there. I wondered whether the two swollen faces were of Mr. Jaggers’s family,

59
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:21,278
and, if he were so unfortunate as to have had a pair of such ill-looking relations,

60
00:05:21,278 --> 00:05:25,919
why he stuck them on that dusty perch for the blacks and flies to settle on, instead

61
00:05:25,919 --> 00:05:33,319
of giving them a place at home. Of course I had no experience of a London summer day, and my spirits

62
00:05:33,319 --> 00:05:38,477
may have been oppressed by the hot exhausted air, and by the dust and grit that lay thick

63
00:05:38,478 --> 00:05:45,319
on everything. But I sat wondering and waiting in Mr. Jaggers’s close room, until I really

64
00:05:45,319 --> 00:05:52,240
could not bear the two casts on the shelf above Mr. Jaggers’s chair, and got up and went out.

65
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:57,637
When I told the clerk that I would take a turn in the air while I waited, he advised me to go round

66
00:05:57,638 --> 00:06:05,919
the corner and I should come into Smithfield. So I came into Smithfield; and the shameful place,

67
00:06:05,919 --> 00:06:12,599
being all asmear with filth and fat and blood and foam, seemed to stick to me. So,

68
00:06:12,599 --> 00:06:16,958
I rubbed it off with all possible speed by turning into a street where I saw the great

69
00:06:16,958 --> 00:06:23,000
black dome of Saint Paul’s bulging at me from behind a grim stone building which a bystander

70
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,039
said was Newgate Prison. Following the wall of the jail, I found the roadway covered with

71
00:06:29,039 --> 00:06:35,118
straw to deaden the noise of passing vehicles; and from this, and from the quantity of people

72
00:06:35,118 --> 00:06:42,840
standing about smelling strongly of spirits and beer, I inferred that the trials were on.

73
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:49,199
While I looked about me here, an exceedingly dirty and partially drunk minister of justice asked me

74
00:06:49,199 --> 00:06:55,199
if I would like to step in and hear a trial or so: informing me that he could give me a front

75
00:06:55,199 --> 00:07:00,278
place for half a crown, whence I should command a full view of the Lord Chief Justice in his

76
00:07:00,278 --> 00:07:06,879
wig and robes,—mentioning that awful personage like waxwork, and presently offering him at the

77
00:07:06,879 --> 00:07:12,958
reduced price of eighteen-pence. As I declined the proposal on the plea of an appointment,

78
00:07:12,958 --> 00:07:18,039
he was so good as to take me into a yard and show me where the gallows was kept,

79
00:07:18,039 --> 00:07:23,159
and also where people were publicly whipped, and then he showed me the Debtors’ Door, out

80
00:07:23,159 --> 00:07:28,519
of which culprits came to be hanged; heightening the interest of that dreadful portal by giving me

81
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:33,520
to understand that “four on ’em” would come out at that door the day after to-morrow at eight

82
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:41,520
in the morning, to be killed in a row. This was horrible, and gave me a sickening idea of London;

83
00:07:41,520 --> 00:07:46,958
the more so as the Lord Chief Justice’s proprietor wore (from his hat down to his boots and up again

84
00:07:46,958 --> 00:07:52,359
to his pocket-handkerchief inclusive) mildewed clothes which had evidently not belonged to

85
00:07:52,359 --> 00:07:58,240
him originally, and which I took it into my head he had bought cheap of the executioner.

86
00:07:58,798 --> 00:08:03,879
Under these circumstances I thought myself well rid of him for a shilling.

87
00:08:03,879 --> 00:08:09,080
I dropped into the office to ask if Mr. Jaggers had come in yet, and I found he had not,

88
00:08:09,080 --> 00:08:14,638
and I strolled out again. This time, I made the tour of Little Britain, and turned into

89
00:08:14,638 --> 00:08:20,240
Bartholomew Close; and now I became aware that other people were waiting about for Mr. Jaggers,

90
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:26,118
as well as I. There were two men of secret appearance lounging in Bartholomew Close,

91
00:08:26,118 --> 00:08:30,400
and thoughtfully fitting their feet into the cracks of the pavement as they talked together,

92
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:34,319
one of whom said to the other when they first passed me, that “Jaggers would do

93
00:08:34,319 --> 00:08:40,679
it if it was to be done.” There was a knot of three men and two women standing at a corner,

94
00:08:40,680 --> 00:08:45,958
and one of the women was crying on her dirty shawl, and the other comforted her by saying,

95
00:08:45,958 --> 00:08:52,879
as she pulled her own shawl over her shoulders, “Jaggers is for him, ’Melia, and what more could

96
00:08:52,879 --> 00:08:59,958
you have?” There was a red-eyed little Jew who came into the Close while I was loitering there,

97
00:08:59,958 --> 00:09:06,240
in company with a second little Jew whom he sent upon an errand; and while the messenger was gone,

98
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:12,519
I remarked this Jew, who was of a highly excitable temperament, performing a jig of anxiety under a

99
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:20,520
lamp-post and accompanying himself, in a kind of frenzy, with the words, “O Jaggerth, Jaggerth,

100
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:28,599
Jaggerth! all otherth ith Cag-Maggerth, give me Jaggerth!” These testimonies to the popularity

101
00:09:28,599 --> 00:09:34,759
of my guardian made a deep impression on me, and I admired and wondered more than ever.

102
00:09:34,759 --> 00:09:40,319
At length, as I was looking out at the iron gate of Bartholomew Close into Little Britain,

103
00:09:40,319 --> 00:09:46,319
I saw Mr. Jaggers coming across the road towards me. All the others who were waiting saw him at the

104
00:09:46,319 --> 00:09:51,840
same time, and there was quite a rush at him. Mr. Jaggers, putting a hand on my shoulder and walking

105
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:57,000
me on at his side without saying anything to me, addressed himself to his followers.

106
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:03,359
First, he took the two secret men. “Now, I have nothing to say to you,” said

107
00:10:03,359 --> 00:10:10,838
Mr. Jaggers, throwing his finger at them. “I want to know no more than I know. As to the result,

108
00:10:10,840 --> 00:10:17,520
it’s a toss-up. I told you from the first it was a toss-up. Have you paid Wemmick?”

109
00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:21,520
“We made the money up this morning, sir,” said one of the men, submissively,

110
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:24,719
while the other perused Mr. Jaggers’s face.

111
00:10:24,719 --> 00:10:27,199
“I don’t ask you when you made it up, or where,

112
00:10:27,199 --> 00:10:31,598
or whether you made it up at all. Has Wemmick got it?”

113
00:10:31,599 --> 00:10:34,559
“Yes, sir,” said both the men together.

114
00:10:34,559 --> 00:10:39,559
“Very well; then you may go. Now, I won’t have it!” said Mr Jaggers,

115
00:10:39,559 --> 00:10:46,638
waving his hand at them to put them behind him. “If you say a word to me, I’ll throw up the case.”

116
00:10:46,638 --> 00:10:51,798
“We thought, Mr. Jaggers—” one of the men began, pulling off his hat.

117
00:10:51,798 --> 00:10:58,599
“That’s what I told you not to do,” said Mr. Jaggers. “You thought! I think for you;

118
00:10:58,599 --> 00:11:02,559
that’s enough for you. If I want you, I know where to find you;

119
00:11:02,559 --> 00:11:08,278
I don’t want you to find me. Now I won’t have it. I won’t hear a word.”

120
00:11:08,278 --> 00:11:12,718
The two men looked at one another as Mr. Jaggers waved them behind again,

121
00:11:12,719 --> 00:11:16,599
and humbly fell back and were heard no more.

122
00:11:16,599 --> 00:11:21,798
“And now you!” said Mr. Jaggers, suddenly stopping, and turning on the two women with

123
00:11:21,798 --> 00:11:29,438
the shawls, from whom the three men had meekly separated,—“Oh! Amelia, is it?”

124
00:11:29,438 --> 00:11:31,477
“Yes, Mr. Jaggers.”

125
00:11:31,478 --> 00:11:34,278
“And do you remember,” retorted Mr. Jaggers,

126
00:11:34,278 --> 00:11:37,798
“that but for me you wouldn’t be here and couldn’t be here?”

127
00:11:37,798 --> 00:11:44,359
“O yes, sir!” exclaimed both women together. “Lord bless you, sir, well we knows that!”

128
00:11:44,359 --> 00:11:47,919
“Then why,” said Mr. Jaggers, “do you come here?”

129
00:11:47,919 --> 00:11:51,199
“My Bill, sir!” the crying woman pleaded.

130
00:11:51,199 --> 00:11:56,438
“Now, I tell you what!” said Mr. Jaggers. “Once for all. If you don’t know that your Bill’s

131
00:11:56,438 --> 00:12:01,558
in good hands, I know it. And if you come here bothering about your Bill, I’ll make an example

132
00:12:01,559 --> 00:12:06,680
of both your Bill and you, and let him slip through my fingers. Have you paid Wemmick?”

133
00:12:06,680 --> 00:12:08,680
“O yes, sir! Every farden.”

134
00:12:08,680 --> 00:12:10,958
“Very well. Then you have done all you have got to

135
00:12:10,958 --> 00:12:17,680
do. Say another word—one single word—and Wemmick shall give you your money back.”

136
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:23,199
This terrible threat caused the two women to fall off immediately. No one remained now but the

137
00:12:23,199 --> 00:12:29,798
excitable Jew, who had already raised the skirts of Mr. Jaggers’s coat to his lips several times.

138
00:12:29,798 --> 00:12:33,000
“I don’t know this man!” said Mr. Jaggers,

139
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:37,508
in the same devastating strain: “What does this fellow want?”

140
00:12:37,508 --> 00:12:42,240
“Ma thear Mithter Jaggerth. Hown brother to Habraham Latharuth?”

141
00:12:42,240 --> 00:12:46,719
“Who’s he?” said Mr. Jaggers. “Let go of my coat.”

142
00:12:46,719 --> 00:12:51,719
The suitor, kissing the hem of the garment again before relinquishing it, replied,

143
00:12:51,719 --> 00:12:55,438
“Habraham Latharuth, on thuthpithion of plate.”

144
00:12:55,438 --> 00:12:59,359
“You’re too late,” said Mr. Jaggers. “I am over the way.”

145
00:12:59,359 --> 00:13:04,319
“Holy father, Mithter Jaggerth!” cried my excitable acquaintance, turning white,

146
00:13:04,319 --> 00:13:07,319
“don’t thay you’re again Habraham Latharuth!”

147
00:13:07,319 --> 00:13:11,718
“I am,” said Mr. Jaggers, “and there’s an end of it. Get out of the way.”

148
00:13:11,719 --> 00:13:17,520
“Mithter Jaggerth! Half a moment! My hown cuthen’th gone to Mithter Wemmick at thith

149
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:24,159
prethent minute, to hoffer him hany termth. Mithter Jaggerth! Half a quarter of a moment!

150
00:13:24,159 --> 00:13:26,958
If you’d have the condethenthun to be bought off from the t’other

151
00:13:26,958 --> 00:13:35,278
thide—at hany thuperior prithe!—money no object!—Mithter Jaggerth—Mithter—!”

152
00:13:35,278 --> 00:13:39,598
My guardian threw his supplicant off with supreme indifference,

153
00:13:39,599 --> 00:13:45,840
and left him dancing on the pavement as if it were red hot. Without further interruption,

154
00:13:45,840 --> 00:13:52,759
we reached the front office, where we found the clerk and the man in velveteen with the fur cap.

155
00:13:52,759 --> 00:13:56,438
“Here’s Mike,” said the clerk, getting down from his stool,

156
00:13:56,438 --> 00:13:59,477
and approaching Mr. Jaggers confidentially.

157
00:13:59,478 --> 00:14:02,520
“Oh!” said Mr. Jaggers, turning to the man,

158
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:05,899
who was pulling a lock of hair in the middle of his forehead, like the Bull

159
00:14:05,899 --> 00:14:11,478
in Cock Robin pulling at the bell-rope; “your man comes on this afternoon. Well?”

160
00:14:11,478 --> 00:14:18,000
“Well, Mas’r Jaggers,” returned Mike, in the voice of a sufferer from a constitutional cold;

161
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:22,478
“arter a deal o’ trouble, I’ve found one, sir, as might do.”

162
00:14:22,478 --> 00:14:25,000
“What is he prepared to swear?”

163
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
“Well, Mas’r Jaggers,” said Mike,

164
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:33,958
wiping his nose on his fur cap this time; “in a general way, anythink.”

165
00:14:33,958 --> 00:14:40,559
Mr. Jaggers suddenly became most irate. “Now, I warned you before,” said he, throwing his

166
00:14:40,559 --> 00:14:45,359
forefinger at the terrified client, “that if you ever presumed to talk in that way here,

167
00:14:45,359 --> 00:14:50,679
I’d make an example of you. You infernal scoundrel, how dare you tell ME that?”

168
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:56,559
The client looked scared, but bewildered too, as if he were unconscious what he had done.

169
00:14:56,559 --> 00:14:59,478
“Spooney!” said the clerk, in a low voice,

170
00:14:59,478 --> 00:15:05,919
giving him a stir with his elbow. “Soft Head! Need you say it face to face?”

171
00:15:05,919 --> 00:15:10,559
“Now, I ask you, you blundering booby,” said my guardian, very sternly,

172
00:15:10,559 --> 00:15:16,159
“once more and for the last time, what the man you have brought here is prepared to swear?”

173
00:15:16,159 --> 00:15:20,400
Mike looked hard at my guardian, as if he were trying to learn a lesson from his face,

174
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:24,118
and slowly replied, “Ayther to character,

175
00:15:24,118 --> 00:15:29,879
or to having been in his company and never left him all the night in question.”

176
00:15:29,879 --> 00:15:34,798
“Now, be careful. In what station of life is this man?”

177
00:15:34,798 --> 00:15:39,319
Mike looked at his cap, and looked at the floor, and looked at the ceiling, and looked at the

178
00:15:39,319 --> 00:15:45,558
clerk, and even looked at me, before beginning to reply in a nervous manner, “We’ve dressed him up

179
00:15:45,559 --> 00:15:52,520
like—” when my guardian blustered out,— “What? You WILL, will you?”

180
00:15:52,520 --> 00:15:56,278
(“Spooney!” added the clerk again, with another stir.)

181
00:15:56,278 --> 00:16:01,319
After some helpless casting about, Mike brightened and began again:—

182
00:16:01,319 --> 00:16:06,919
“He is dressed like a ’spectable pieman. A sort of a pastry-cook.”

183
00:16:06,919 --> 00:16:09,879
“Is he here?” asked my guardian.

184
00:16:09,879 --> 00:16:14,599
“I left him,” said Mike, “a setting on some doorsteps round the corner.”

185
00:16:14,599 --> 00:16:17,478
“Take him past that window, and let me see him.”

186
00:16:17,478 --> 00:16:23,559
The window indicated was the office window. We all three went to it, behind the wire blind, and

187
00:16:23,559 --> 00:16:29,559
presently saw the client go by in an accidental manner, with a murderous-looking tall individual,

188
00:16:29,559 --> 00:16:35,478
in a short suit of white linen and a paper cap. This guileless confectioner was not

189
00:16:35,478 --> 00:16:42,000
by any means sober, and had a black eye in the green stage of recovery, which was painted over.

190
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:46,719
“Tell him to take his witness away directly,” said my guardian to the clerk,

191
00:16:46,719 --> 00:16:53,118
in extreme disgust, “and ask him what he means by bringing such a fellow as that.”

192
00:16:53,118 --> 00:16:57,919
My guardian then took me into his own room, and while he lunched, standing, from a sandwich-box

193
00:16:57,919 --> 00:17:03,159
and a pocket-flask of sherry (he seemed to bully his very sandwich as he ate it),

194
00:17:03,159 --> 00:17:08,919
informed me what arrangements he had made for me. I was to go to “Barnard’s Inn,” to young

195
00:17:08,919 --> 00:17:14,958
Mr. Pocket’s rooms, where a bed had been sent in for my accommodation; I was to remain with

196
00:17:14,958 --> 00:17:21,798
young Mr. Pocket until Monday; on Monday I was to go with him to his father’s house on a visit,

197
00:17:21,798 --> 00:17:27,798
that I might try how I liked it. Also, I was told what my allowance was to be,—it

198
00:17:27,798 --> 00:17:32,638
was a very liberal one,—and had handed to me from one of my guardian’s drawers,

199
00:17:32,638 --> 00:17:37,080
the cards of certain tradesmen with whom I was to deal for all kinds of clothes,

200
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:43,080
and such other things as I could in reason want. “You will find your credit good, Mr. Pip,” said

201
00:17:43,080 --> 00:17:49,159
my guardian, whose flask of sherry smelt like a whole caskful, as he hastily refreshed himself,

202
00:17:49,159 --> 00:17:53,598
“but I shall by this means be able to check your bills, and to pull you up if I find you

203
00:17:53,599 --> 00:18:01,159
outrunning the constable. Of course you’ll go wrong somehow, but that’s no fault of mine.”

204
00:18:01,159 --> 00:18:04,359
After I had pondered a little over this encouraging sentiment,

205
00:18:04,359 --> 00:18:09,359
I asked Mr. Jaggers if I could send for a coach? He said it was not worth while,

206
00:18:09,359 --> 00:18:14,519
I was so near my destination; Wemmick should walk round with me, if I pleased.

207
00:18:14,520 --> 00:18:19,559
I then found that Wemmick was the clerk in the next room. Another clerk was rung down

208
00:18:19,559 --> 00:18:24,719
from upstairs to take his place while he was out, and I accompanied him into the street,

209
00:18:24,719 --> 00:18:30,438
after shaking hands with my guardian. We found a new set of people lingering outside,

210
00:18:30,438 --> 00:18:36,080
but Wemmick made a way among them by saying coolly yet decisively, “I tell you it’s no use;

211
00:18:36,080 --> 00:18:39,798
he won’t have a word to say to one of you;” and we soon got clear of them,

212
00:18:39,798 --> 00:18:43,000
and went on side by side. Chapter 21.
